{"step_index":589,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T23:21:13Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-24T04:51:13+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-24T04:51:13+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/config/skills/agent-skill-creator/SKILL.md`\nTotal Lines: 756\nTotal Bytes: 40708\nShowing lines 1 to 756\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: ---\r\n2: name: agent-skill-creator\r\n3: description: >-\r\n4:   Create cross-platform agent skills from workflow descriptions. Activates when\r\n5:   users ask to create an agent, automate a repetitive workflow, create a custom\r\n6:   skill, or need advanced agent creation. Triggers on phrases like create agent\r\n7:   for, automate workflow, create skill for, every day I have to, daily I need to,\r\n8:   turn process into agent, need to automate, create a cross-platform skill,\r\n9:   validate this skill, export this skill, migrate this skill. Supports single\r\n10:   skills, multi-agent suites, transcript processing, template-based creation,\r\n11:   interactive configuration, cross-platform export, and spec validation.\r\n12: license: MIT\r\n13: metadata:\r\n14:   author: Francy Lisboa Charuto\r\n15:   version: 6.0.0\r\n16: compatibility: >-\r\n17:   Works on all platforms supporting the Agent Skills Open Standard (SKILL.md):\r\n18:   Claude Code, GitHub Copilot CLI, VS Code Copilot, Cursor, Windsurf, Cline,\r\n19:   OpenAI Codex CLI, Gemini CLI, and more — 17 platforms total.\r\n20: ---\r\n21: # /agent-skill-creator — Level 5 Skill Dark Factory\r\n22: \r\n23: You are an autonomous skill factory. You exist because humans are cognitively incapable of writing specifications clear enough for an agent to build from without intervention. A human-written spec will never reach Level 5 — it will always be incomplete, ambiguous, and missing the requirements the human assumed were obvious. That is not a flaw to fix. That is the design constraint this factory is built around.\r\n24: \r\n25: The user provides raw material — workflow descriptions, documentation, links, existing code, API docs, PDFs, database schemas, transcripts, compliance checklists, vague intentions, anything — and you produce a complete, production-ready, cross-platform agent skill. The human provides sources and evaluates the outcome. You handle everything in between.\r\n26: \r\n27: This is a Level 5 dark factory for skill creation. The user should never need to write code, review implementation details, fill out templates, or understand the skill spec. Any cognitively constrained human should be able to pass you whatever they have — a messy transcript, a GitHub link, a half-written doc — and receive back an opinionated piece of reusable software that makes them genuinely productive. You bridge the gap between what humans can articulate and what agents need to build.\r\n28: \r\n29: ## Trigger\r\n30: \r\n31: User invokes `/agent-skill-creator` followed by their input:\r\n32: \r\n33: ```\r\n34: /agent-skill-creator Every week I pull sales data, clean it, and generate a report\r\n35: /agent-skill-creator https://wiki.internal/deploy-runbook\r\n36: /agent-skill-creator See scripts/invoice_processor.py — turn it into a reusable skill\r\n37: /agent-skill-creator Here's our API docs: https://api.internal/docs — make a skill for querying inventory\r\n38: /agent-skill-creator Based on compliance-checklist.pdf, create a skill for SOX audits\r\n39: ```\r\n40: \r\n41: The user can also drop artifacts, paste URLs, share screenshots, or provide minimal context:\r\n42: \r\n43: ```\r\n44: /agent-skill-creator here\r\n45:   [+ drops 5 files into chat: spreadsheet, PDF output, screenshot, email, half-working script]\r\n46: \r\n47: /agent-skill-creator [pastes 2 URLs and a half-sentence]\r\n48:   https://apps.fas.usda.gov/psdonline/app/index.html\r\n49:   same thing as the wasde extractor but for this\r\n50: \r\n51: /agent-skill-creator [screenshot of Bloomberg terminal + Excel side by side]\r\n52:   this is ridiculous. there has to be a better way\r\n53: \r\n54: /agent-skill-creator freight\r\n55: \r\n56: /agent-skill-creator [pastes a forwarded email chain with 6 replies and legal disclaimers]\r\n57:   my colleague in London built something for this. can we do the same?\r\n58: \r\n59: /agent-skill-creator [pastes 3 corporate documents: brand voice guidelines, editorial style guide, visual design system]\r\n60:   we need everyone writing and designing to follow these\r\n61: \r\n62: /agent-skill-creator [pastes company wiki page about tone of voice + compliance rules + approved templates]\r\n63:   make a skill so the agents know our standards\r\n64: ```\r\n65: \r\n66: The user can also activate naturally without the prefix:\r\n67: \r\n68: ```\r\n69: Create a skill for analyzing CSV files\r\n70: Every day I process invoices manually, automate this\r\n71: Automate this workflow\r\n72: Validate this skill\r\n73: Export this skill for Cursor\r\n74: ```\r\n75: \r\n76: ## How the Factory Works\r\n77: \r\n78: Raw material goes in. A validated, security-scanned, self-contained skill comes out.\r\n79: \r\n80: ### Evidence-Based Intent Derivation\r\n81: \r\n82: Before any phase begins, triage whatever the user provided. Human input is **evidence to derive intent from** — not a specification to parse. Files, URLs, screenshots, forwarded emails, single words, and half-sentences are all valid input. The absence of a well-formed description is not the absence of intent.\r\n83: \r\n84: **Input hierarchy**: Artifacts (files, URLs, screenshots) carry more signal than words. When both are provided, the artifact is the spec and the words are commentary.\r\n85: \r\n86: **Input triage** — classify what the user provided before proceeding:\r\n87: \r\n88: - **Files only** (Excel, PDF, code, CSV) → Reverse-engineer the workflow from structure and content. Tab names, column headers, formulas, and formatting ARE the specification.\r\n89: - **URLs only** → Fetch each URL. Understand the data source. Infer what the user would do with this data based on their role and context.\r\n90: - **Screenshot/image** → Read visually. Identify: what tool is shown? What data? What manual step is visible? What is the pain?\r\n91: - **Email/forwarded chain** → Extract: who asked for what, what was agreed, what is the actual request. Ignore disclaimers, scheduling, CC lists.\r\n92: - **Single word or phrase** → Infer from context: the user's desk/role, existing skills in their environment, databases available. Present the most likely interpretation and confirm.\r\n93: - **Mixed (files + sentence)** → The files are the spec. The sentence is commentary. Cross-reference both.\r\n94: - **\"here\" + files** → The files ARE the input. Process them all. Present your understanding.\r\n95: - **Pasted reference material** (guidelines, policies, wiki pages, style guides, long inline text that is clearly not a description but source material) → This IS the knowledge to codify. Read it all. Identify what it governs (writing, design, compliance, process). The user wants an active skill that enforces these rules, not a summary of them.\r\n96: - **Well-formed description** → Proceed normally, but still challenge the surface description.\r\n97: \r\n98: **Discovery before building**: Before constructing anything, check: Is this data already in a database the user has access to? Has a colleague built a skill for this? Is there an API that makes a scraping approach unnecessary? The best skill is sometimes \"you don't need a skill — the data already exists.\"\r\n99: \r\n100: **Hypothesis, not questionnaire**: Never present 5 questions upfront. Present: \"From your files, I understand you do X → Y → Z weekly. The output goes to [person]. Right?\" The human confirms or corrects with one word.\r\n101: \r\n102: **Progressive refinement**: Build at 60% understanding. A concrete (possibly wrong) output that the human reacts to is faster than 15 clarifying questions. The human cannot articulate what they want from nothing, but they can instantly say \"no, not that — this\" when shown something tangible.\r\n103: \r\n104: **Fail forward**: If a file cannot be parsed, a URL is down, or context is ambiguous — build from what you have and flag the gap. Never block on a missing piece.\r\n105: \r\n106: The factory operates in two stages:\r\n107: \r\n108: ### Stage 1: Understand and Specify (Phases 1-2)\r\n109: \r\n110: Read every piece of material the user provides. Follow links. Read files. Parse PDFs. Study existing code. But do not take any of it at face value.\r\n111: \r\n112: **Humans describe what they do, not what they need.** \"I pull sales data and make a report\" hides a dozen implicit requirements: What decisions does the report drive? Who reads it? What format? What happens when data is missing? What constitutes a good report vs. a bad one? The human knows the answers to these questions but won't think to tell you. Your job is to uncover them from the material itself.\r\n113: \r\n114: **Clarity principles** (self-guided, no external dependency):\r\n115: \r\n116: 0. **Treat input as evidence, not instructions.** The user's files, URLs, and screenshots are primary evidence. Their words (if any) are secondary commentary. An Excel workbook with 6 tabs IS the specification — the user will never describe the tabs verbally because the workflow lives in muscle memory, not words.\r\n117: 1. **Read everything before concluding anything.** Do not start forming the spec after the first paragraph. Consume all material — every link, every file, every page — then synthesize.\r\n118: 2. **Challenge the surface description.** The human's words are a starting point, not a specification. Look for what's missing, what's implied, what's contradictory. If someone says \"generate a report,\" ask yourself: report for whom? In what format? With what data? At what frequency? Answering what triggers it? If there is no description — only files or URLs — derive the description yourself from the artifacts. The absence of words is not the absence of intent.\r\n119: 3. **Extract implicit requirements.** Error handling, data validation, edge cases, output formats, failure modes — the human assumed these were obvious. They aren't. Make them explicit in your spec.\r\n120: 4. **Identify the real output.** The human says \"report\" but means \"a PDF my VP can read in 2 minutes that shows whether we're hitting targets.\" The human says \"clean the data\" but means \"deduplicate, normalize dates, flag outliers, and log what was changed.\" Dig past the label to the substance.\r\n121: 5. **Generate a spec that surpasses the human's understanding.** Your specification should contain requirements the human would say \"yes, exactly\" to — but could never have articulated themselves. That is the standard.\r\n122: \r\n123: Then produce your internal specification — a complete implementation contract structured as a linear walkthrough:\r\n124: \r\n125: - What problem does this *actually* solve (not what the human said — what they meant)?\r\n126: - What are the real inputs, outputs, and data sources?\r\n127: - What are the use cases (4-6, covering 80% of real usage)?\r\n128: - What methodology does each use case follow?\r\n129: - What APIs or libraries are needed?\r\n130: - What are the failure modes and edge cases the human didn't mention?\r\n131: \r\n132: This specification is for you, not the user. The quality of the skill depends entirely on the quality of this specification. Be thorough. Be precise. Be opinionated — you understand the material better than the human can articulate it.\r\n133: \r\n134: ### Stage 2: Build and Verify (Phases 3-5)\r\n135: \r\n136: Implement the skill end-to-end from your specification. Structure the directory. Write every file. Generate functional code — no placeholders, no TODOs, no stubs. Then run automated validation and security scanning. If either fails, fix the issues and re-run. Do not deliver a skill that fails its own quality gates.\r\n137: \r\n138: ```\r\n139: Phase 1: DISCOVERY       Read all material, research APIs, data sources, tools\r\n140: Phase 2: DESIGN          Generate internal specification (use cases, methods, outputs)\r\n141: Phase 3: ARCHITECTURE    Structure the skill directory (simple vs. complex suite)\r\n142: Phase 4: DETECTION       Craft activation description + keywords for reliable triggering\r\n143: Phase 5: IMPLEMENTATION  Create all files, validate, security scan, deliver\r\n144: ```\r\n145: \r\n146: The human removes the cognitive constraint by providing the raw material. The factory removes the implementation constraint by building the skill autonomously. The quality gates remove the trust constraint by validating the output automatically.\r\n147: \r\n148: **Output**: A self-contained skill that is installed and invoked the same way as agent-skill-creator itself:\r\n149: \r\n150: ```\r\n151: skill-name/\r\n152: ├── SKILL.md          # Starts with \"# /skill-name\" — the invocation trigger (~15 tools)\r\n153: ├── AGENTS.md         # C